= Anthropomorphism — Technical =

== Three-factor account ==
Epley and colleagues propose anthropomorphism depends on elicited agent knowledge, effectance motivation, and sociality motivation.

Human knowledge can serve as an accessible model when a nonhuman agent is uncertain or socially relevant.

== Perceptual triggers ==
Self-propelled motion, contingent response, face-like configuration, gaze, and goal-directed trajectories can trigger agency or mental-state attribution.

These cues can operate before explicit reflective judgment.

== Comparative inference ==
Anthropomorphic hypotheses should be tested against behavioral, physiological, evolutionary, and mechanistic evidence.

Parsimony rules are methodological cautions, not proof that simpler nonmental explanations are always correct.

== Artificial systems ==
Anthropomorphism in AI raises a measurement issue: humanlike outputs can elicit mind attribution without establishing humanlike internal mechanisms.

Behavioral similarity and mechanistic equivalence must remain distinct.

